    "narrative": "Our offensive outlook for May 15th is characterized by high-floor matchups across the board, with **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** serving as our clear focal point. Boasting the highest score on the board at 78.89, **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** draws a favorable matchup against Andrew Abbott; despite the southpaw opponent, his efficiency remains untouchable due to his elite base projection and the stability of the #2 spot in the order. Similarly, **Geraldo Perdomo** (73.81) and **Tyler Freeman** (72.83) benefit from excellent park factors and favorable matchups against Kyle Freeland and Merrill Kelly, respectively. Notably, **Tyler Freeman** holds extra appeal due to Kelly\u2019s slower delivery, which provides a high-probability environment for our focus on stolen bases. **Will Smith** (72.24) remains a mandatory start against the soft contact of Jack Kochanowicz, with his #1 spot and dynamic platoon advantage locking him into the C1 slot.\n\nThe optimizer faced its toughest decision in the utility ranks, ultimately favoring the reliability of our starting outfield over the upside of **Masyn Winn**. While **Masyn Winn** posted a solid 57.24, he was sidelined in favor of the current outfield configuration because his lower batting position and the neutral park factors attached to his matchup against Michael Wacha failed to overcome the collective scoring advantage provided by **Lawrence Butler**\u2014who benefits from a massive 13% dynamic platoon boost against Tyler Mahle\u2014and the consistent production expected from **Brendan Donovan**. **Daulton Varsho** (58.71) sits on the edge of our starting threshold; while his raw score is lower, his dynamic platoon edge against Brenan Hanifee provides enough separation to keep him in the lineup over the surging **Jeremiah Jackson**, whose low #9 batting order expectation significantly penalized his overall efficiency score.\n\nWe must remain vigilant regarding the \"Lineup Pending\" status across the roster, as 